# Architectures for a quantum random access memory

Vittorio Giovannetti$^1$, Seth Lloyd$^2$, Lorenzo Maccone$^3$

$^1$ NEST CNR-INFM & Scuola Normale Superiore, Piazza dei Cavalieri 7, I-56126, Pisa, Italy.
$^2$ MIT, Research Laboratory of Electronics and Dept. of Mechanical Engineering, 77 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA.
$^3$ QUIT - Quantum Information Theory Group, Dipartimento di Fisica "A. Volta", Università di Pavia, via A. Bassi 6, I-27100 Pavia, Italy.

## Abstract

A random access memory (RAM) is a device that, when interrogated, returns
the content of a memory location in a memory array. A quantum RAM (qRAM)
allows one to access superpositions of memory sites. Any RAM with an
$n$-bit address must contain $O(2^n)$ two-bit logic gates, but we show
that with the bucket-brigade architecture only $O(n)$ two-qubit
physical interactions need be *activated* per memory call.

## I. Description of the protocol

An $n$-bit address selects one of $N = 2^n$ memory cells. The qRAM
transformation is

$$ \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \alpha_k \left| k \right\rangle_Q
     \; \longmapsto \;
     \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \alpha_k \left| k \right\rangle_Q
                                \left| f_k \right\rangle_A , \tag{1} $$

where $f_k$ is the (classical or quantum) content of cell $k$.

### Fanout architecture

The $k$-th index qubit controls $2^k$ bifurcations at level $k$ of the
binary switching tree, giving $\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} 2^k = N - 1$ active
couplings per memory call and requiring simultaneous quantum control
over an exponential number of connections.

### Bucket-brigade architecture

Each of the $2^n - 1$ nodes of the binary tree hosts a qutrit with basis
$\{|0\rangle, |1\rangle, |\bullet\rangle\}$, initialized in
$|\bullet\rangle$. The $n$ index qubits are sent sequentially. A qutrit
in $|\bullet\rangle$ that receives $|b\rangle$ becomes $|b\rangle$
(this is the load unitary $U_s$); a qutrit in $|0\rangle$ or $|1\rangle$
routes any subsequent signal into its left or right child, respectively.
After all $n$ qubits have been sent, exactly $n$ qutrits have been
activated (one at each level), and $2^n - (n+1)$ remain in
$|\bullet\rangle$.

The bus qubit is then routed to the leaf, C-NOT'ed against the memory
cell there, sent back, and $U_s^\dagger$ is applied to disentangle the
address register from the tree. The net transformation on $(Q,A)$ is
exactly (1).

### Error scaling

Per-switch error rate $\varepsilon$ gives a total error per call
$n\varepsilon = (\log_2 N)\varepsilon$; e.g. $\varepsilon = 10^{-2}$
yields 10% total error at $N = 2^{10}$ and 20% at $N = 2^{20}$.

## II. Physical implementations

II.A — Quantum-optical fanout qRAM (polarization + PBS + trapped ions).
II.B — Solid-state fanout qRAM (superconducting flux qubits, controlled
phases via circuit-QED style couplers).
II.C — Bucket-brigade qRAM: three-level atoms at each node, with a
photonic bus. The paper gives explicit unitaries for the load ($U_s$)
and its inverse ($U_s^\dagger$).

## III. Discussion

Bucket-brigade qRAMs are potentially scalable to $N \sim 2^{100}$
without extensive quantum error correction, provided
$\varepsilon \lesssim 1/n$. For quantum-content memory cells, C-NOT is
replaced by SWAP and the memory array is left entangled with $(Q,A)$.

